# hwut-feature-doc.dat -- WHAT THE FEATURES ARE (spec 43, intend/43 T-c,
# T-g, T-p, T-s). One block per feature, headed '[[<id>]]' -- or
# '[[<id> to <audience>, ...]]', '[[<id> not <audience>, ...]]',
# '[[<id>:retired]]'; '[[CUSTOMERS]]' declares the audiences. A block: an
# optional 'documents:' line (paths from this file's directory, ','
# between), then the prose -- its first line the title, the rest the doc.

[[min-pulse]]
No debounced pulse is shorter than the 5 ms threshold.

[[one-press-one-pulse]]
One press of the button gives exactly one high pulse, however much the contact bounces.

[[glitch-named]]
A debounced pulse below the threshold is listed with its start and length and marked '!!'.

[[lifecycle]]
A replay walks opened -> active <-> frozen -> closed; a frozen account takes no withdrawal, a closed one nothing.

[[seq-contiguous]]
Sequence numbers in the replay run 1, 2, 3, ... without gap or repeat.

[[no-overdraft]]
The replayed balance never drops below zero, even when two clerks withdraw at the same moment.

[[conflict-redecided]]
Of two racing withdrawals the loser meets a version conflict and is decided again on the fresh state.

[[race-named]]
A store without the version check shows the race as a repeated sequence number and a balance below zero.

[[exactly-once]]
Every job put into the queue is done exactly once, whatever the interleaving of four workers.

[[causal-order]]
No job is taken before it was queued, and nothing is queued after the queue was closed.

[[lease-extended]]
A worker that touches its lease keeps a slow job; it is not redelivered.

[[double-work-named]]
A job done twice because its lease ran out is named, with the redelivery and the stale acknowledgement.

[[one-order]]
Every thread takes account locks in one global order (by name): the lock-order graph has no cycle.

[[cycle-named]]
A lock-order inversion is reported as a cycle of the graph, each link with the threads that hold and take it.

[[lock-order-money-conserved]]
Whatever the interleaving of the transfers, the total of all balances stays 300.

[[evicts-lru]]
A new key in a full cache evicts the entry whose last use lies furthest back.

[[hit-refreshes]]
A hit counts as a use: the entry moves to the head of the list.

[[put-refreshes]]
Putting a key that is present updates its value and counts as a use.

[[wrong-eviction-named]]
An eviction that is not the least recently used entry is marked '!!' with the entry that should have gone.

[[same-lines]]
A live conversation holds the same requests and answers on every run, whatever their order.

[[answer-after-request]]
The server answers a request only after it was made.

[[answered-once]]
Every request is answered exactly once, and bye counts the answers.

[[potpourri-blind]]
A potpourri accepts a transcript whose answer came before its request: it cannot see order, by design.

[[early-answer-named]]
The pype law names an answer that came before its request.

[[preemption]]
In every tick the ready job with the highest effective priority runs; a lower one waits as '-'.

[[deadlines-met]]
A periodic task set with utilisation 0.75 under rate-monotonic priorities misses no deadline.

[[miss-shown]]
A task set beyond the CPU's capacity shows where it runs out: the missed deadlines are marked and listed.

[[inversion-shown]]
Without a mutex protocol a medium task can hold off the high one that waits for the mutex: the miss is shown.

[[inheritance-bounds]]
With priority inheritance the holder of the mutex runs at the waiter's priority, and 'bus' meets its 8-tick deadline.

[[retained-first]]
A subscriber's first delivery on a topic is the value retained when it subscribed.

[[publish-order]]
A subscriber gets a topic's values in the order they were published.

[[silence-after-gone]]
Once unsubscribe has returned, nothing more is delivered to that subscriber on that topic.

[[none-lost]]
Every value published while subscribed is delivered, or purged at unsubscribe -- never silently lost.

[[late-delivery-named]]
A delivery after unsubscribe has returned is named as a breach for its subscriber and topic.

[[retry-backoff-window-law]]
The delay before retry k lies in [w/2, w], w = min(5000 ms, 100 ms x 2^k), whatever is drawn.

[[jitter-spread]]
Inside each window the delay is really drawn: over 1000 sequences the delays spread across at least 80 % of the half window.

[[decorrelated]]
Clients that fail together do not retry together: no retry within 5 ms of another client's.

[[herd-named]]
A policy without jitter shows as identical client rows, and is named a thundering herd.

[[whole-bytes]]
A CS-low phase is decoded into whole bytes on MOSI and MISO, most significant bit first.

[[rising-edge]]
A bit is sampled at each rising SCK edge while CS is low, and at no other time.

[[cs-frames]]
Each CS-low phase is one transaction; its bytes do not run into the next one.

[[truncated-named]]
A transaction that ends inside a byte is reported with its whole bytes and the bits left over; no byte is invented.

[[rfc-edges]]
Every state change of the endpoint is an edge of the RFC 793 diagram, with the segments it prescribes.

[[reaches-closed]]
A connection closed by both sides ends in CLOSED on both, through TIME_WAIT on the side that closed first.

[[closing-state]]
FINs that cross on the wire take both ends through CLOSING.

[[violation-named]]
A transition outside RFC 793 is named with the edge the RFC prescribes instead.

[[token-bucket-window-law]]
In any window of one second, at most 15 messages pass (10 per second plus a burst of 5).

[[under-rate-all-pass]]
Traffic below 10 messages per second is never dropped.

[[burst-after-quiet]]
After a quiet spell the bucket holds 5 tokens and no more: a storm passes 5 plus the refill.

[[token-bucket-breach-named]]
A one-second window with more than 15 messages sent is named with its count.

[[chart-walk]]
The lamps change only along the reference chart: green, yellow, all-red, the other road's green, and round.

[[min-green]]
NS keeps green for at least 10 s before a waiting car on EW is served.

[[all-red]]
Between one road's red and the other road's green, all lamps stay red for at least 2 s.

[[two-greens-shown]]
A lamp change the chart does not admit -- such as a second green -- is named, and the timeline shows it.

[[traffic-light-breach-named]]
A breach of the chart or of a minimum duration is named with its time and the state it happened in.

[[bytes-as-sent]]
The receiver delivers exactly the bytes the sender sent, in order.

[[mid-bit-look]]
The receiver samples every bit once, one bit time apart, starting in the middle of the start bit.

[[framing-error]]
A frame whose stop bit is low is delivered flagged as a framing error, and the next frame is read again.

[[glitch-rejected]]
A spike on the idle line that is gone at the middle of the start bit is ignored.

[[clock-tolerance]]
A sender clock 3 % fast is read correctly; at 12 % the drift is visible in the look row.

[[garbage-shown]]
Bytes that differ from those sent are reported with '!!', even when every stop bit was high.

[[reset-on-silence]]
The watchdog resets the MCU at the very millisecond the task has not kicked for 300 ms.

[[no-reset-while-kicked]]
While the task kicks within 300 ms, the watchdog never resets, however close a gap comes.

[[window]]
With a 60 ms window, a kick sooner than 60 ms after the previous one resets at once.

[[missed-reset-named]]
A reset the specification predicts and the watchdog does not perform is named as a missed reset.

[[same-options]]
The parser of benchctl, its --help, its README and its man page name the same set of long options; an option missing from a place, or named only there, is a finding.

[[same-defaults]]
Every place that states a default states the parser's default.

[[todo-owner]]
Every work note reads 'TODO(owner): text'; FIXME, XXX, HACK and an anonymous TODO are reported.

[[cpp-line-comments]]
C++ files use '//' comments only; a '/* */' comment in a .cpp or .hpp file is reported.

[[no-dead-code]]
No comment holds commented-out code (text ending in ';', '{' or '}'); each one is reported with its line.

[[message-law]]
Every commit message keeps the house law -- 'type(scope): subject' of at most 50 characters without a final '.', a blank second line, body lines of at most 72 characters, a Signed-off-by trailer -- and each breach is named with its commit.

[[read-is-documented]]
Every configuration key the code reads -- INI key or environment variable, in Python or shell -- has a row in the README's configuration table; INI keys also a line in config.example.ini.

[[documented-is-read]]
Every key in the README's configuration table is read by some code.

[[switch-complete]]
Every switch over motor_state_t names every enum value in a case; a 'default:' does not count, and every hole is shown in the coverage matrix.

[[gcc-agrees]]
The holes found by reading the source equal the warnings of gcc -Wswitch-enum, one for one.

[[unique]]
An error code value is defined once in the whole code base, across C, JavaScript and Python.

[[in-range]]
Each error code lies in the range of the component that defines it (fw 1000..1999, web 2000..2999, tools 3000..3999).

[[documented]]
Every defined code stands in docs/errors.md under its name, and every documented code is defined somewhere.

[[prefix]]
Every macro #defined in <module>.h starts with the module prefix MODULE_; a macro without it is shown in the prefix ruler.

[[guard]]
Each header tests and defines the guard <MODULE>_H as its first two directives and ends with #endif; any other guard is named.

[[complete]]
Every key of the source catalogue en.json stands, non-empty, in every other language catalogue; a gap is a cell of the matrix.

[[placeholders]]
A translation carries exactly the placeholders of its source text; a translated or lost placeholder is shown, source against translation.

[[no-stale]]
No language catalogue holds a key the source catalogue no longer has.

[[downward-only]]
No file includes a file of a layer above its own; every upward include is named with file, line and the two layers.

[[every-file-layered]]
Every source directory has a layer in layers.ini.

[[exact-rebuild]]
After touching any one source file, make rebuilds exactly the targets whose real inputs include it: nothing missed, nothing extra.

[[declared-deps]]
The prerequisites declared in the Makefile equal the files the compiler reads (gcc -MM); an undeclared or never-read file is named.

[[reachable]]
Every target of the Makefile is reachable from 'all' or named in STANDALONE; a target reached from nowhere is reported by name.

[[phony]]
Every target whose recipe does not write $@ is listed in .PHONY; one that is not is reported by name.

[[spdx-present]]
Every source file, in any language, carries an SPDX-License-Identifier within its first three lines (after a '#!' line).

[[spdx-identical]]
The identifier and the copyright line equal LICENSE-HEADER.txt word for word, written as comments of the file's own language; each deviation is shown against the line that should stand.

[[sequence]]
Migrations are numbered 0001, 0002, ... without a gap, and no number is used twice.

[[reversible]]
Every up migration has a down migration, and the down drops exactly the tables, columns and indexes the up creates.

[[hygiene-report]]
Each file is checked byte by byte for tab indentation, trailing blanks, CR LF, a BOM, non-ASCII in code and a missing final newline; each breach is shown with its first line made visible.

[[hygiene-self]]
The test applications of this showcase area keep the same rules, whatever their number.

[[check-value]]
crc32 over "123456789" prints the CRC-32 check value CBF43926, results only on stdout.

[[exit-codes]]
crc32 exits 0 when every file was read, 1 when one could not be read, 2 for a bad command line; --help exits 0.

[[streams]]
Every complaint of crc32 goes to stderr, prefixed 'crc32: '.

[[keeps-going]]
A file crc32 cannot read does not stop it from reading the others.

[[cli-matrix-violation-shown]]
A build that breaks the command-line contract is caught: each row of the matrix names the rule it breaks.

[[examples]]
The codec encodes the textbook frames to the textbook encodings.

[[cobs-roundtrip-round-trip]]
Decoding an encoded frame gives back the frame, byte for byte, for any frame up to 1000 bytes.

[[no-zero]]
An encoded frame contains no 0x00 byte.

[[overhead]]
Encoding adds at most 1 + n/254 bytes to a frame of n bytes.

[[cobs-roundtrip-violation-shown]]
A decoder that mishandles a full 254-byte block is caught by long and edge frames, while short random frames alone would miss it.

[[typed]]
Every valid CSV row becomes one JSON object, keys in schema order, numbers as numbers, an empty optional field as null.

[[quoting]]
Quoted CSV fields keep their commas and quotes; a doubled quote is one quote.

[[all-or-named]]
A row that breaks the schema is left out, every broken rule is named as file:line: column: what, and the exit status is 1.

[[csv-json-violation-shown]]
A partner export that breaks the schema is caught: each rejected row stands beside the rule it broke.

[[wall-time]]
A daily job runs at its local wall time; where that time does not exist, at the first minute after the gap.

[[one-per-day]]
A daily job runs exactly once per local day, at the first of two equal wall times, so runs are 23 to 25 hours apart.

[[month-end]]
A monthly job on the 31st runs on the month's last day where the month is shorter, never in the next month.

[[dst-schedule-violation-shown]]
A scheduler that skips, doubles or spills a run is caught: the calendar marks the day and the table names the broken rule.

[[precedence]]
Expressions group as in mathematics: * before + and -, ^ before *, + - * to the left, ^ to the right, -2^2 = -(2^2).

[[error-position]]
A parse error says what was expected and points at the column where it was not found.

[[agrees]]
On random expressions over + - * ^ and parentheses the parser's value equals Python's value of the same text.

[[expr-parser-violation-shown]]
An operator table with a wrong associativity is caught: its tree leans the wrong way and random expressions disagree with Python.

[[steady]]
At rest, the filtered altitude stays within 0.18 m RMS of the truth.

[[settling]]
After a 3 m elevator ride the estimate is back within 0.30 m of the truth in at most 2.5 s and stays inside for a second.

[[smoothing]]
At rest, the estimate's error typically (median of 40 rides) has less than 0.45 of the spread of the raw sensor's error.

[[kalman-baro-violation-shown]]
A mistuned filter is caught: the plot shows the lag or the wobble, and the sweep of 40 noisy rides breaks the stated rule.

[[log-summary-counts]]
The summary counts requests and errors per host and per minute, with each host's 95th percentile latency.

[[restarts]]
Every restart is listed with the process that exited and the one that replaced it.

[[no-false-alarm]]
A single restart raises no alert.

[[crash-loop-alert]]
A process restarted more than 3 times within 300 s raises a CRASH LOOP alert naming host and process.

[[unreadable-lines]]
Lines that cannot be read are named by line number and left out of every count.

[[exact-cents]]
Net, VAT and gross of every invoice line equal the exact amounts rounded half-up to the cent.

[[adds-up]]
Every printed invoice line adds up: net + VAT = gross, to the cent.

[[money-vat-violation-shown]]
A tool that rounds in binary floating point is caught: the page shows its value beside the reference and counts its wrong cents.

[[fit-within-spec]]
Every calibration point lies within 0.15 K of the fitted Steinhart-Hart curve.

[[noise-tolerated]]
A re-measured table (resistance noise 0.05 %) gives coefficients within 5 % and fitted temperatures within 0.15 K of the accepted fit.

[[ntc-fit-violation-shown]]
One mistyped calibration row is caught: the residual bars show the outlier and the tilt it gives every other point.

[[parallel-count-counts]]
Every file is counted once, and the total is the sum of the per-file counts.

[[order-law]]
Each file's lines come as start, count, done, and the total comes last, after every worker has finished.

[[parallel-cap]]
At most 4 files are counted at the same moment.

[[parallel-count-violation-shown]]
A build that runs every file at once is caught: the law names the breach of the 4-file cap.

[[correct]]
count_fields() counts the fields of a CSV line right, quoted commas included.

[[linear]]
count_fields() takes time linear in the line length: fitted time ~ size^k over 2..32 KiB, k <= 1.5.

[[throughput]]
count_fields() processes at least 100 MB/s on a 32 KiB line.

[[perf-budget-violation-shown]]
A quadratic build is caught by its fitted exponent and its throughput, while its field counts stay right.

[[by-key]]
Records sorted by dept come out in dept order.

[[stable]]
Records with the same dept keep their input order.

[[sort-stability-violation-shown]]
A sorter that reorders equal keys is caught: the tags inside a dept stop increasing, and random arrays count the reorderings.

[[same-tree]]
After mirroring, DST holds exactly SRC's paths with SRC's content, whatever the file names.

[[untouched]]
A file whose content already matched is not rewritten; its modification time stays.

[[only-dst]]
Mirroring changes nothing outside DST: neither SRC nor a sibling directory.

[[tree-mirror-violation-shown]]
A backup step that skips dot files, keeps stale files or rewrites everything is caught; the three trees show where.

[[accurate]]
Every conversion between the 8 pressure units is within 1e-9 (relative) of the exact value from the units' definitions.

[[unit-convert-round-trip]]
Converting a pressure there and back, through any unit, returns it within 1e-14 (relative).

[[unit-convert-violation-shown]]
A factor table with rounded factors is caught by the accuracy check, though its round trips still pass.

[[width]]
No wrapped line is longer than the width (40 columns).

[[whole-words]]
Wrapping never cuts a word: the words out are the words in, in order.

[[justified]]
Every line but the last of a paragraph is flush on both edges.

[[word-wrap-violation-shown]]
A tool that breaks a wrapping rule is caught, and the page marks each offending line (RAGGED, CUT, LONG).

[[drawn-interleaving]]
Each interleaving is given as timeline strings and shown as a lane picture: per tick the step of each task and the balance.

[[bank-race-money-conserved]]
With the lock, cash out plus balance equals the opening balance in every drawn interleaving and every enumerated schedule, and exactly one withdrawal is paid.

[[lost-update-named]]
Without the lock, an interleaving that opens the read-write window pays both and names the amount created by the lost update.

[[law-per-timeline]]
The event stream of each drawn timeline is judged by a pype law (capacity, FIFO, none lost, all finished).

[[never-overfull]]
With the guarded put, the law is kept in every drawn timeline, the consumer-first start-up included.

[[overfull-named]]
With check-then-put, a timeline in which both producers see one free slot overfills the buffer; tick and item are named.

[[one-outcome]]
Every order of the reply's and the cancel's steps (C(4,2) = 6, the step counts measured) is enumerated and run, one row each.

[[one-callback]]
With compare-and-set, every order calls the user once and releases the connection slot once.

[[two-callbacks-named]]
With check-then-set, the orders in which both look while pending tell the user twice and release twice; they are marked and counted.

[[month-lengths]]
Days 1 to the month's length are valid, the day after it is not, for every one of the twelve months.

[[out-of-range]]
Month 0, month 13, day 0 and day 32 are invalid in every year.

[[leap-rule]]
February 29 is valid in 2000 and 2024, and invalid in 1900, 2023 and 2100.

[[date-valid-wrong-named]]
A point where the validator disagrees with the calendar is marked '!' and named as a date.

[[heartbleed-reproducible]]
The 3000 fuzz inputs follow from one seed; any case is reproduced by seed and case number.

[[operators-spread]]
Each of the six mutation operators is applied to exactly 500 cases (select_unchosen).

[[no-overread]]
The bounded walker reads no byte outside its block for any of the 3000 cases.

[[overread-reproduced]]
A walker that trusts the length byte shows its overreads per operator and its first reproducer in hex.

[[accepts-grammar]]
The shell answers OK to every generated sentence of its command grammar.

[[full-coverage]]
With one SelectionMarker per site (a nonterminal in its context), the budget computed from the grammar exercises every alternative in every context.

[[bug-found]]
The covering walk generates 'parity none' and the refusal of release 1.4 is named.

[[hole-named]]
A uniform walk of the same budget leaves alternatives ungenerated at some site; each hole is named with its context.

[[first-rule]]
hdr_check reports the first broken rule in the order T(<4) V H L T Y, else ok.

[[space-from-file]]
The tested space is the cartesian product of the lists in space.conf, minus the points its 'where' prunes; both counts are printed.

[[header-matrix-wrong-named]]
A frame whose letter differs from the spec is marked 'X!' and named with its fields.

[[interleavings-all-interleavings]]
All interleavings of two reservations, of the step counts measured alone (here C(6,3) = 20), are generated and run, one row each; a task left unfinished is reported.

[[never-oversold]]
With the conditional update, every interleaving confirms exactly one order and ends with stock 0.

[[oversold-rows-named]]
With check-then-act, every interleaving that sells the last item twice is marked and counted.

[[priority-drawn]]
Two timelines that differ in one priority digit place a reader before or after the writer's fill in the same tick.

[[whole-object]]
With late publication every caller reads port 8080 in every drawn timeline and every enumerated schedule.

[[built-once]]
The configuration is built exactly once, also with two initialising callers.

[[half-object-named]]
With early publication the reader that goes first in the window reads port None, named as a half-initialised object.

[[model-agrees]]
For one capacity of every class (1, powers of two, others, 64) and every load, the FIFO answers every push and pop as a bounded queue.

[[cell-seeds]]
Each cell of the capacity x load space draws its operations from its own seed, derived from the cell's name.

[[divergence-named]]
A cell where FIFO and model differ shows the first differing operation; the first such cell shows its history.

[[front-end-answers]]
A HwutRunner application answers --hwut-info, typos, indices, manual mode and the interactive protocol from its choice map; a hand-written argv switch answers none of them.

[[leak-shown]]
In a sequential session, a choice whose output depends on state left by an earlier choice is marked DIFFERS with its lines.

[[byte-exact]]
In a forking session, every choice prints byte-exactly what it prints in a process of its own.

[[exact-in-range]]
Where a + b fits into int32, sat_add returns exactly a + b.

[[clamped]]
Where a + b leaves int32, sat_add returns INT32_MAX above and INT32_MIN below.

[[sat-add-wrong-named]]
A wrong cell of the boundary matrix is marked '!' and named with operands, result and the clamped sum.

[[alive]]
The dead seeds of MINSTD (0 and its aliases) are detected as runs whose draws are all equal.

[[first-draws-spread]]
With the splitmix seeding, no run is dead and the step between consecutive runs' first draws is spread over all ten values.

[[linear-named]]
With the multiplicative seeding, the lattice of first draws is named as lock-step: one step value holds more than a quarter of the pairs.

[[dead-and-striped]]
With consecutive seeds, run 0 is dead and the first draws step by +1 from run to run; both are named.

[[ints-everywhere]]
next_int of DeterministicStream gives the same 100000 draws under Python 3.11, 3.12, 3.13, in C and in JavaScript.

[[random-not-portable]]
The standard 'random' is shown beside it: same randint here, a moving API, and no port outside CPython.

[[gauss-libm]]
gauss of DeterministicStream differs in the last bit between math libraries; the count of differing values is stated.

[[gauss-printed]]
The print formats under which all gauss values agree on every producer are named.

[[bank-race-all-schedules]]
Every order of the two withdrawals' steps is enumerated and judged; the schedules that break the invariant are counted and the first one is drawn.

[[consumer-first]]
A timeline in which the consumer takes the first step, before any producer registered, keeps the law with the guarded unit.

[[every-rule]]
Every rule of the spec is reached by at least one point of the space; the points per rule are printed.

[[lazy-init-all-schedules]]
Every order of two callers' steps is enumerated and judged; the schedules that break the requirement are counted and the first one is drawn.

[[index-wraps]]
Every cell walks until 512 bytes went in, so every index passes two wraps of a byte, under every load.

[[car-served]]
A car on EW gets EW green within 20 s: the controller does not starve the side road.

[[same-bases]]
A feature converted from BED to GFF covers exactly the bases the BED interval covers.

[[vcf-ref]]
A single-base BED feature written as VCF has POS = BED start + 1 and the reference base there as REF.

[[roundtrip]]
BED -> GFF -> BED returns every interval unchanged, over a seeded sweep of 300 random intervals.

[[misplaced-shown]]
A converter that forgets the shift is shown feature by feature on the ruler, with the wrong bases, the moved interval and the wrong REF.

[[detected]]
The encoding of a FASTQ file is detected from the range of its quality characters: below ';' is Phred+33, '@' and above with a top beyond 'J' is Phred+64, anything else is called ambiguous.

[[same-q]]
The same reads written as Phred+33 and as Phred+64 decode, each with its detected offset, to the same Q for every base.

[[bars]]
Per read, a column per base shows the quality as bars at Q 40, 30, 20, 10, 0 and marks negative Q.

[[fastq-phred-breach-shown]]
A reader that assumes Phred+64 on Phred+33 data is caught: negative Q values are marked and counted.

[[orders-shown]]
The sum of the same 100000 doubles is shown for six orders, each with its error against the exact sum and in units of u * sum|x|.

[[kahan-bound]]
sum_accurate() (Kahan) is within 2 u * sum|x| of the exact sum.

[[partials-any-order]]
Each worker's partial sum is reported exactly as a recomputation gives it, in whatever order the workers finish.

[[arrival-total]]
The parallel total is one of the totals the possible arrival orders of the partials give; all of them are listed.

[[float-summation-breach-shown]]
Kahan's summation compiled with -ffast-math is caught: its error exceeds the bound, its row equals the plain loop's.

[[glider-moves]]
Under B3/S23 the glider is, after 4 generations, its own shape moved one cell down and one right.

[[oscillator-still-life]]
The blinker returns after 2 generations and differs after 1; the block never changes.

[[torus-returns]]
On an 8 x 8 torus the glider is back home after 32 generations, with 5 cells in every generation; the old '23/3' notation is read as survival/birth.

[[game-of-life-breach-shown]]
A mistyped rule or a torus that does not wrap at two edges is caught: each broken law is named and counted.

[[symbols-survive]]
Every gene symbol of the list comes out of an import and export spelled as it went in.

[[mangled-named]]
A symbol the import turned into a date or a number is marked in the symbol table and counted by kind.

[[renamed-symbols]]
The HGNC 2020 symbols (SEPTIN2, MARCHF1, DELEC1, ...) pass the smart import without becoming dates.

[[artifact-any-order]]
The file of significant genes the unit exports starts with its header and holds exactly the expected rows, whatever order the unit writes them in.

[[matrix-shown]]
The DP matrix and the alignment of the classic pair GATTACA / GCATGCU are shown column-exact.

[[score-recomputes]]
Every printed alignment spells both sequences, and its score recomputed column by column equals the reported score, under a scheme where mismatch and gap cost the same and one where they differ.

[[optimal]]
Over 200 seeded random pairs, the reported score equals the best score of a brute force over all alignments.

[[needleman-wunsch-breach-shown]]
A boundary initialised with (i - 1) * gap is caught: pairs whose reported score is not their alignment's are drawn with both scores.

[[six-frames]]
The six reading frames of a sequence are drawn with every amino acid under the middle base of its codon, and the minus strand pairs with the plus strand base by base.

[[standard-code]]
translate() uses the standard genetic code: every one of the 64 codons agrees with NCBI table 1, and an ORF of all 61 sense codons has no internal stop.

[[involution]]
revcomp(revcomp(s)) == s over 500 random sequences.

[[antiparallel]]
revcomp(a + b) == revcomp(b) + revcomp(a) over 500 random pairs: the result is read 5' to 3'.

[[involution-blind]]
A complement without reversal keeps the involution law; only the antiparallel law and the pairing row reveal it.

[[revcomp-translate-breach-shown]]
A wrong table or a missing reversal is shown: '!' cells in the codon grid, internal stops named, 'x' in the pairing row, the broken law counted.

[[curves-drawn]]
S, I and R over 120 days are drawn as an ASCII plot, one column per 2 days.

[[curve-recorded]]
The infected curve stays within distance 1.0 (days, persons per 1000) of the recorded reference curve.

[[population-kept]]
S + I + R stays N within 1e-9 N, and S never goes negative.

[[peak-on-time]]
The peak of I lies within 0.25 days of the peak of an independent fine-step oracle.

[[final-size]]
The end state obeys the Kermack-McKendrick final-size relation S_end = S(0) exp(-R0 R_end / N) within 1e-4 N.

[[sir-model-breach-shown]]
A daily explicit-Euler step is caught by the peak-timing law although it conserves N exactly; the law table names each broken law.

[[range-table]]
Values inside, on and beyond both limits of int16 (half a unit beyond included) are converted; each result is set against the right answer.

[[flight-strip]]
Two BH curves over H0 .. H0+40 s are converted second by second and drawn as a strip: right, clamped, wrapped, error, stopped.

[[conversion-guarded]]
An out-of-range value is clamped to the limit and flagged; the SRI never stops and never delivers a wrapped value.

[[failure-named]]
An Operand Error, a wrapped value or a stopped SRI is marked in the table and in the flight strip.

[[index-space]]
Searches over array lengths 2^30 .. 2^31-1 and keys from the bottom to beyond the top are run and drawn as one matrix.

[[every-search-right]]
Every key is found at its index and the absent key reports its insertion point, for every length up to 2^31-1.

[[overflow-named]]
A search whose midpoint leaves the array is named with low, high, the wrapped sum and the midpoint.

[[threads-named]]
Each seat's thread and its two forks are named as analogies; every later line about a seat uses the same thread and fork values.

[[cycle-found]]
A deadlock is found as a cycle in a consistent snapshot of the waits-for graph, not by waiting for a timeout.

[[cycle-drawn]]
The waits-for cycle is drawn as a chain P -[fork]-> P that closes on its start, with holder and waiter of every fork.

[[everyone-eats]]
With resource ordering no waits-for cycle forms and every philosopher eats all his meals.

[[source-checked]]
The C source of the function is read as text: statements, the 'if' that governs each, and every goto and label.

[[flow-drawn]]
The function's control flow is drawn statement by statement, indented by structure, with reachability and the kind of each jump.

[[all-reachable]]
No statement is unreachable: after an unconditional goto or return only a label follows at the same level.

[[unreachable-named]]
Every unreachable statement and every doubled goto line is named with its line number.

[[forgery-refused]]
A ServerKeyExchange signed with the wrong key, or with altered parameters, is refused.

[[forgery-shown]]
A forged ServerKeyExchange that the client accepts is shown as 'returns 0' and counted.

[[whole-space]]
Every triple of the boundary basis -1 0 1 2 3 4 MAX, all permutations, is classified and counted in a truth-by-answer matrix.

[[classified-right]]
Every triple gets the right answer: degenerate (a+b=c) and impossible triples are invalid, overflow of a+b changes nothing.

[[mismatch-named]]
A wrong answer stands off the diagonal and is named with its sides, the permutations it hits, and the truth.

[[tenth-shown]]
The register's value of 0.1 is shown bit for bit, with its shortfall per tenth of a second.

[[drift-table]]
Calculated time and its inaccuracy are tabled at uptimes 0 .. 100 h, with the gate interval error and the gate shift in metres.

[[gao-reproduced]]
The model's inaccuracy matches GAO's published table (IMTEC-92-26, Appendix II) to its four decimals.

[[gate-stays]]
The time interval that places the range gate stays within 1 ms of the truth over 100 hours of uptime.

[[gate-drift-shown]]
A gate interval error that grows with uptime is shown as a growing bar of metres and judged by the 1 ms law.

[[table-drawn]]
The upper half of the SRT lookup table is drawn: remainder estimate by divisor column, each cell's digit, unreachable cells, and cells that lack their 2.

[[famous-divisions]]
Nicely's 4195835 / 3145727 and 5505001 / 294911 are divided and set against the exact quotient.

[[fuzz-against-exact]]
A seeded fuzz divides 4000 uniform pairs and 1000 at-risk pairs per divisor column and compares each quotient with the exact one.

[[always-right]]
Every quotient is right to a relative error below 2^-50, famous, uniform and at-risk alike.

[[pentium-fdiv-wrong-named]]
Every wrong quotient is listed with its operands, the exact value and how many significant digits are still right; failures are counted per divisor column.

[[peterson-dekker-all-schedules]]
Every schedule of 10 atomic steps of two processes (1024) that enter, leave and re-enter forever is run and drawn as one grid.

[[state-closure]]
All interleavings are walked breadth-first, merged by system state, until a length adds no state: every reachable state is judged.

[[exclusion-holds]]
No reachable state has both processes in their critical section, on any entry; processes do enter, and enter again.

[[violation-drawn]]
Reachable states with both processes inside are counted; the shortest schedule to one is drawn step by step as two lanes.

[[same-answers]]
The rule answers 'match or not' exactly as '"=" in the input' on 400 seeded random inputs: a rewrite keeps the rule's meaning.

[[growth-measured]]
The time to decide an input without '=' is measured for n = 25 .. 400 and fitted as time ~ n^k; the table shows it, the exponent is judged.

[[within-budget]]
Decision time grows at most linearly (k <= 1.5) and stays below 1 ms for inputs up to 400 characters.

[[budget-broken-shown]]
Super-linear growth of the decision time is shown by the table's bars and stated as a law on the fitted exponent.

[[edit-interleavings]]
One edit X-ray -> electron at every tick 0 .. 10, before and after the setup task's step of that tick, is run against the setup and 'beam on'.

[[window-drawn]]
Each run is drawn as two lanes over the ticks, operator and setup task, with the current and turntable the beam fired with.

[[never-mismatched]]
Whatever the moment of the edit, the beam fires, and fires with current and turntable set for the same mode.

[[overdose-named]]
A run that fires the X-ray current at a turntable in the electron position is marked as a mismatch, and counted.

[[boundary-table]]
The clock is shown against true UTC at the epoch, at 10^9 s and second by second around 2038-01-19 03:14:07 UTC, then +1 min, +1 day, +1 year, and far instants before 1901 and up to 9999.

[[time-continues]]
Past 2^31-1 seconds the clock shows the right UTC instant: time never runs backwards.

[[wrap-named]]
A row where the clock wraps is marked, with the raw (negative) seconds and the date it shows instead.

[[loop-traced]]
The year loop for 2008-12-31 is traced iteration by iteration; a repeated (year, days) state is marked as the proof that it cannot end.

[[edge-days]]
Dec 30, Dec 31, Jan 1 and Feb 29 of every year 1980 .. 2040 are converted, each in its own process, and drawn as a strip.

[[every-day-converts]]
Every edge day 1980 .. 2040 and every day of 2000, 2008, 2009 and 2100 converts to its right date, and the loop always ends.

[[hang-named]]
A day on which the loop does not end is marked 'H' and counted.

[[hwut-kills-it]]
hwut itself, running a device test with caps { timeout_sec = 1 } on the shipped firmware, reports 'killed; wall-clock cap exceeded'.

[[ambiguous-said]]
A file whose quality characters fit both encodings (lowest character 59..63, or all of them in 64..74) is called ambiguous, not guessed.

[[rule-pinned]]
Every one of the 512 3x3 neighbourhoods gives the next state B3/S23 demands for its centre; the rule as applied is shown as born/stays per neighbour count.

[[sheet-shown]]
Every step of the fuel sheet is shown with its unit: density, mass on board, mass required, mass to add, litres to add.

[[density-plausible]]
The density that turns dripstick litres into mass is a density of jet fuel, about 0.775 to 0.840 kg/L, in whatever unit the sheet writes it.

[[gimli-glider-unit-converted]]
A density given in lb/L is used as such: the sheet computes in pounds and the fuel comes out right.

[[fuel-enough]]
After refuelling as the sheet says, the fuel on board is at least the 22,300 kg the flight plan requires.

[[shortfall-named]]
Fuel on board below the requirement is shown as a bar against the required one, and the shortfall is named in kg.

[[amd-table]]
Every AMD event is listed with the impulse the file states, the impulse navigation reads from it, and the impulse the thrusters gave.

[[impulse-as-fired]]
The AMD file gives each impulse as fired: over the model cruise navigation takes 10.0 lbf*s in all, whatever unit the file names.

[[mars-climate-orbiter-unit-converted]]
An impulse the file tags in lbf*s is read as its value in N*s: the unit travels with the number and the reader converts it.

[[periapsis-safe]]
The impulse navigation models keeps the periapsis at Mars at or above the 80 km the MIB took as survivable.

[[underread-named]]
An event read smaller than fired is marked '!!' with its ratio, and the unmodelled impulse is named.

[[periapsis-lost]]
The periapsis an under-read file leads to is shown against the 80 km survivable altitude.

[[calendar-swept]]
The clock's calendar agrees with Python's datetime on Feb 28/29, Mar 1 and Dec 31 of every year 1902 .. 2400, and at far instants up to 9999.

[[part-identified]]
The capacity announced by a decoded JEDEC ID is the part's datasheet capacity: 128 Mibit for a W25Q128.

[[every-pulse-counted]]
Every pulse that reaches TIME1, CDUT or CDUS is counted; none is lost.

[[priority-order]]
Waiting counters are served one per memory cycle, by priority TIME1 > CDUT > CDUS.

[[cycle-budget]]
Counting takes at most 100 ms of memory cycles per second of flight; the Apollo 11 radar pulses take 150 ms.

[[lost-named]]
A counter that loses pulses is named with the number lost and how many of its pulses shared a cycle with a higher-priority pulse.

[[gauges]]
A gauge reading in psi, mmHg, inHg or Torr, shown in hPa, is within 0.001 Pa of the value pint derives from the unit's definition.

[[gauges-off]]
The old firmware table puts every gauge reading off by more than 0.001 Pa; pint's units show it.

[[dirty-cow-race-all-interleavings]]
Every interleaving of the writer's steps with the discarder's step, of the counts measured alone, is run, one row each; an actor left unfinished is reported.

[[recheck-guard]]
With the write-time re-check, every interleaving writes to the private copy: no write reaches the shared read-only object.

[[losing-rows-named]]
Without the re-check, every interleaving in which the copy is discarded between fault and write is marked and counted, and the shortest is drawn as a timeline.

[[wrap-tabled]]
Each counter is sampled around the moment it runs out of bits, and a table shows the true time, the raw counter and the decoded time.

[[gcu-wrap-named]]
The Boeing 787 GCU's signed 32-bit centisecond counter decodes to minus 248.55 days at 2^31 cs and is marked as running backwards into fail-safe.

[[gps-wrap-named]]
The 10-bit GPS week decodes 1999-08-22 and 2019-04-07 as 1980-01-06, and both rollovers are marked as running backwards.

[[y2k-wrap-named]]
A two-digit year decodes 2000 as 1900, marked as running backwards.

[[time-monotonic]]
With the counters widened (64-bit centiseconds, 13-bit week, four-digit year) no decoded time runs backwards.

[[network-drawn]]
Every switch of the network is drawn as a strip of 60 seconds: where a reset began, where it was down, where it served.

[[first-steps-told]]
The first resets are told one per line: when, which switch, and the message that reset it.

[[cascade-named]]
With the 'break' as shipped, a neighbour busy with a recovering switch's first message resets on its second, and the resets cascade; they are counted.

[[no-cascade]]
With the 'break' gone, the switch that reset first is the only one that resets.

[[headline-transaction]]
The transaction of block 74638 -- two outputs of 92233720368.54277039 BTC -- is shown with its exact sum, the int64 sum the client saw and the release's answer.

[[boundary-matrix]]
Every pair of outputs from 0, 1 satoshi, MAX_MONEY, MAX_MONEY + 1, 2^62, the 2010 value and 2^63 - 1, against an input of 0.5 BTC and of MAX_MONEY, is checked against the exact rule.

[[wrap-accepted]]
Release 0.3.10 accepts every pair whose int64 sum wraps to no more than the input, the 2010 transaction among them.

[[every-invalid-refused]]
Release 0.3.11 accepts no invalid transaction of the space and refuses no valid one.

[[instance-matrix]]
Every template instance is drawn as its 21 fields, '*' a wildcard and 'c' a criterion, in the order of delivery.

[[validator-verdict]]
Each instance carries the content validator's word, accepted or refused with the reason.

[[crash-named]]
The instance with a criterion on field 21 passes the validator and makes the interpreter read input 21 of 20: a crash, counted.

[[no-crash]]
No instance makes the interpreter read an input the sensor does not supply.

[[machines-drawn]]
2000 machines, each with a process id in 1 .. 32768 and 64 bits of entropy, are drawn from DeterministicStream(2008), the same on every run.

[[shared-keys-shown]]
The number of distinct keys and the keys held by more than one machine are shown.

[[space-enumerated]]
The key of every process id 1 .. 32768 without entropy is computed, and the machines whose key it holds are counted.

[[collisions-named]]
With the entropy line removed, machines share keys and every machine's key stands in the attacker's table.

[[keys-distinct]]
With the entropy stirred in, the 2000 keys are distinct and none stands in the table of process-id keys.

[[release-table]]
The release each of the eight servers runs is listed, and the number of releases deployed is bound.

[[shares-per-server]]
Per server, the shares ordered and sent for the same three parent orders are tabled, with a bar of the shares sent.

[[overfill-named]]
The server left on the old release reads the reused flag as Power Peg and sends shares beyond the parent orders, counted as overfill.

[[no-overfill]]
No server sends more shares than the parent orders ask for.

[[queries-tabled]]
Seven queries around 2016-12-31 23:59:60 UTC are tabled with their true start and end, the clock's readings, the measured round-trip time and the outcome.

[[negative-named]]
A round-trip time measured on the wall clock across the leap second comes out negative and is counted.

[[panic-named]]
A negative round-trip time given to the back-off ('int63n', as Go's rand.Int63n) panics, and the panic is shown in the query's row.

[[durations-positive]]
Measured on the monotonic clock, every round-trip time is the true one and positive, and no query panics.

[[descent-strip]]
The descent is drawn reading by reading where an event happens or a sensor, a latch or the engine changes: time, altitude, sensors, latches, engine.

[[premature-cutoff-named]]
A touchdown indication latched from the leg-deployment transient at 1500 m stops the engine when sensing is enabled at 40 m, and the altitude and the speed of the fall are named.

[[cutoff-at-touchdown]]
With the latches cleared when sensing is enabled, the engine stops on the real touchdown, at 0 m.

[[names-tabled]]
Every name of the space is shown with its canonical key taken once and taken twice, '!!' where the two differ.

[[one-account-two-keys]]
Pairs of names registered as two accounts whose keys meet when taken twice are listed.

[[not-idempotent-named]]
Folding the case before NFKC lets modifier letters become capitals after the fold: their keys change on the second pass, and they meet the plain accounts.

[[idempotent]]
NFKC, casefold, NFKC gives every name a key that taking it again leaves unchanged; no two accounts meet.

[[call-stack-drawn]]
Every withdraw and every payment is drawn as a call stack, a call made from inside a payment one level deeper.

[[ledger-drawn]]
Per member what it put in, what it received and what it is still owed, and what the contract holds, are drawn as a ledger.

[[reentrancy-drains]]
Paying before zeroing the balance lets the payee call again and be paid again until the contract is empty; the excess and the members left unpaid are counted.

[[effects-first]]
Zeroing the balance before paying makes the reentrant call find nothing owed; every member is paid exactly what it put in.

[[reentry-refused]]
A lock refuses a withdraw while one is running; every member is paid exactly what it put in.

[[months-tabled]]
The exact and the published index stand side by side at every month end of 23 months, with their difference drawn as a bar.

[[drift-named]]
An index truncated to three decimals on every trade falls ever further below the exact one, by hundreds of points, and the deviation is named.

[[drift-bounded]]
An index rounded to three decimals on every trade stays within 1 point of the exact one.

[[qualifier-first:retired]]

[[function-names:retired]]

[[class-names:retired]]

[[global-tone:retired]]

[[local-tone:retired]]

[[dither-reproducible:retired]]

[[dither-violation-shown:retired]]
